# Libraries
Tamil title: நூல் நிலையங்கள்

Address delivered by Anna on 23 January 1950, under the chairmanship of Tamil scholar Thiru. Vi. Ka., at the second anniversary of the Podhigai Library

Beloved Chairman and comrades,

You have invited one described as an atheist to speak. The day has come when people with differences can meet and talk instead of separating. We agree along many paths and differ only along a few. Knowing our heartfelt unity, I accepted the library's invitation without hesitation.

Tamil scholar Thiru. Vi. Ka. explained the meaning of Podhigai well, then said another chairman might have been sought. But is not Thiru. Vi. Ka. himself the leader who moves within Podhigai? No one else could better chair this meeting.

He first demonstrated that one could speak from a platform in good Tamil, and write beautifully in Tamil about politics. In politics he is a storm and in Tamil a gentle breeze. He has crafted subtle commentaries and prepares excellent works for the future world.

I rejoice that Tamil Nadu has found a place beneath the cool pavilion called Podhigai. In ancient times, libraries were not maintained properly and many palm-leaf manuscripts scattered because they were not protected.

Before printing, palm leaves were an excellent writing medium, and we praise the intelligence that developed them. Likewise we praise the inventor of the wooden wheel among travellers who once carried loads on animals, and the person who first placed an iron axle through it. We praise the builders of towers and moats, and the warriors who fought wild animals, cleared forests and made settled lands in the age of monarchy.

An oil lamp once stood in a temple tower; today there is electric light. Change suited to time must occur. In place of towers, our age also builds factory chimneys. In one sense, antiquity and modernity struggle.

We heard a song ask, “How did you decline, Tamil?” The answer is that knowledge declined. That decline crippled people's minds. Influences and assaults from other peoples entered, and knowledge stagnated. Friends founded this library to remove that stagnation.

When I discover in a library a book I have never seen, I feel extraordinary joy. A book is another person's thought collected for us to read.

What would it be like for a Madras resident not to know how to use a telephone or send a telegram, or to see a ship in the harbour and ask whether a house is floating?

Science has advanced to a high point. In the West, technology allows one both to hear and see a distant speaker; the OCR destroys the English term, but the idea anticipates visual telecommunication. The world has arrived at our doorstep.

I want good books that teach modern science. New books should contain sound ideas. Compilations of the best ideas in major old works would greatly benefit people.

Would it not be better for books useful to life in this world to circulate among the public, rather than only books about this world and another, or the relation between the individual soul and the supreme soul?

Government could collect books from every party and field and establish a good library with capital of Rs 1,000 or Rs 2,000. Many sound books could be prepared on branches of administration and knowledge.

An English professor whose name the OCR renders as “Jim” says one should know something about everything and everything about one thing. We cannot gain complete knowledge by reading Tamil alone; we should read excellent books in other languages too. To acquire history, read history.

Many reliable compilations should appear. They must not fabricate falsehood. We rejoice when a book supplies an idea we searched for in vain. Scholars can unite, plan and prepare good works.

How would I organise a library? A book of falsehood would have no place. Can books that sing devotional songs and teach “life is illusion; becoming dust is certain” cultivate reason? Let such books remain in devotional halls. People do not need only books directing them toward life's impermanence.

Passages saying the world is illusion and everything God's test may have themes and meanings, and you will have seen forceful debates about them on street verandas. But visit an English home abroad and one hears science discussed, not the illusory world. In a Tamil home one hears that whoever is not moved by the *Tiruvacakam* will be moved by no other text.

Ask whether a Tamil home has the *Tevaram* or *Tiruvacakam*: it will. Ask for a book about Paris, Stalin, Truman or science: it will not.

People know devotees but not the power of nature. They had opportunities to study nature, but directed attention elsewhere. Ask about Podhigai, the Indra festival, the *Silappatikaram*, ancient battlefields or moats: most will not know. Few study Sangam literature or have even handled the *Silappatikaram*. Our people do not lack intelligence; they fail to direct it toward valuable books.

I do not say ancient ideas must be excluded. Collect the ideas of Tamil works into new books, even including views foreign to us. If a story contains falsehood, its preface should say, “This is a tale invented in that age.”

As Ramalinga Swamigal speaks of squeezing three separate fruits together with sugar and powdered sugar candy, let the best in Tamil books be pressed and combined. We shall welcome, consume, understand and enjoy it.

We need many books that mix contemporary knowledge, offer intellectual flavour and benefit the world. Many people know nothing of the *Akananuru* and *Purananuru*. Their best ideas should be compiled for the public. Good books will awaken people unaware of the wider world.

Our homes may lack those classics but contain *Alli Arasani Malai*, *Bhojan Charitram*, large-print tales of Vikramaditya, *ammanai* songs and laments. People know little of Kovalan, Kannagi, Madhavi's mind, or the hardships suffered by poets. Yet prefaces boldly say Mariamman aided an author or wrote on his tongue with a trident during sleep, giving him poetic power.

The new world must now be given in book form. A story of Vishnu reclining on the ocean of milk may have suited another age. A five-year-old eats a snack openly while walking home; at eighteen or twenty he seeks a proper place. Humanity has childhood and then an age of thought. Can people advance merely by singing devotional refrains at home?

Every book needs a good preface stating truth without fabrication. If it rests on imagination, say so. Give the author's life and period. Do not say Minakshi appeared in the thousand-pillared hall and bestowed poetry; say the poet learned his subject thoroughly.

We inspect old manuscripts and tools in museums but do not adopt them unchanged. Likewise, read old ideas without necessarily practising them. Ashoka's weapon and Shivaji's tiger claw belonged to their time. Wearing Shivaji's claw today will not make us Shivaji.

The editor of *Kalki* once pictured Bharati in a forest pursued by a tiger, saying, “Do not leap; Narasimha is in my heart,” whereupon the tiger stopped. Does this suit the present? What benefit do such pictures and verses give?

Would we believe that a singer instantly gained musical grace when a teacher winked? If Thiru. Vi. Ka. speaks about music, we understand that he studied and developed musical ability. We believe R. P. Sethu Pillai lectures through careful research. We would not believe milk on his desk was “milk of wisdom” that magically produced scholarship.

A rational person now recognises truth and progress. Would anyone today believe Vikramaditya ruled six months in a city and six in a forest, governed Ujjain by Mahakali's grace, or leapt from one body to another?

Tamil Nadu has many books that make the world laugh. One story says Vasuki left a pot suspended in a well when Tiruvalluvar called, rope and vessel freezing in place. Another says she fetched a lamp at noon without asking why. A rope, pulley, pot and person drawing water are natural; a suspended pot proving chastity is meaningless. Praise other virtues if one wishes to honour her.

Village editions of the *Tirukkural* repeat such stories in their prefaces. I do not demand deletion; label them as tales invented in the medieval period about Valluvar's wife Vasuki.

A renaissance has begun. Tamil linguistic consciousness and Dravidian social consciousness can no longer live apart; breeze and storm have joined. Cooperative effort is needed.

Tiruvalluvar has entered the public forum and greatly helps general knowledge. The world honours the *Tirukkural*. Everyone must read it, and every library should contain it.

People should also study foreign history. Governor-General Rajagopalachari recently launched a ship named for V. O. Chidambaram Pillai and spoke of Hindu maritime trade, Hindu kings crossing seas, Mughal ships and Shivaji, but not Tamil naval power.

Does he not know Tamil maritime history? He does. He spoke of Hindu kings because the world recognises that language, while people beyond the Vindhyas know little of Tamil. We must make works that bring Tamil distinction to the wider world. Politicians caught in the political whirl may not manage it; scholars such as Sethu Pillai should.

Many know nothing of Australia but describe Brahmaloka as if they visited it. They cannot name three kinds of aircraft, but know Adisesha, Vishnu's cosmic sleep, Shiva, Parvati, Yama, his buffalo, noose, and Chitragupta's ledger.

Such stories enter children's minds through cradle tales and early schooling. Geography, history, heroes and people who advanced the world are not taught well. People know little of Columbus, Vasco da Gama, scientific innovators, the inventor of the umbrella or builders of ships.

They do not know those persecuted for scientific revolution, Socrates' cup of poison, or a figure the OCR calls “Pope” who was shot for speaking for human welfare. Nor do they know people tortured, burned, exiled, or torn from family for ideas. Good history gives young minds new sinew.

Before me stands a microphone. Many called its inventor mad: a thick wire with a hollow object through which many could hear one speaker. Scientific investigators were mocked by spouses, separated from parents or divided from families. Yet without knowing them, people sing *Tevaram* and *Tiruvacakam* into the microphone.

At Mettur, wild floodwater is stored and converted into electrical power that gives light without flame. Do people understand it? They know little of Kolar Gold Fields workers labouring underground in narrow, unbearable heat.

At the Tiruvottiyur match factory, wood becomes matchsticks; a machine rejects any stick improperly coated with sulphur paste. It seems to compete with the human brain. People know the relation between soul and supreme soul but not the machine or the dangers faced by its inventors.

Thousands gather in Muthialpet for Murugan carried on a peacock, and women run for perfumed sacred ash sold at different prices. Yet they do not know the wonders scientists create to enrich life.

The future must prepare good books about scientists and place them before the public. Every household should establish a small library. Families spend on saris and goods during pilgrimages to Kanchipuram, Tiruvannamalai and Arudra festivals; reduce such expense and buy books.

Our homes have kitchens but no room or shelf for books. There may be a Pillaiyar picture, almanac, *Vinayagar Agaval* and the milkman's account, but no English-Tamil dictionary, atlas or history book. Lack of money is no excuse when thousands are spent on jewellery, ornate saris and unnecessary wedding goods. Allocate part of that spending to books.

Books have not spread widely in Tamil Nadu. English publishers print hundreds of thousands and make the world their market. A Tamil work by Thiru. Vi. Ka. may print only one or two thousand copies; Sethu Pillai's books similarly, while Maraimalai Adigal reportedly prints no more than a thousand.

We call English a barbarian or low language, yet its books sell by the lakh in Kannada, Malayalam, Marathi regions, Eurasia and among Black peoples across the world. Tamil authors print books and keep unsold copies at home, losing encouragement.

Only two publications sell widely here: the almanac and railway guide. Tamil books should sell like them. The Podhigai Library report mentions two hundred novels. Let those novels move into homes, leaving their library space for large dictionaries, books that impart knowledge, explanations of world innovations and research works.

I praise this institution for adopting the name Podhigai, the ancient Tamil mountain. I hope that by gathering at Podhigai you will gain Tamil's sweetness and good benefit.

---NOTES---
The source identifies this as Anna's address on 23 January 1950 at the second anniversary of the Podhigai Library, chaired by Thiru. Vi. Ka.

The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim. The visual-telecommunication term in Image 3, the English educational maxim's attribution, and one persecuted historical figure in Image 10 are OCR-uncertain and not silently normalised.

Devotional stories and verses are quoted and criticised within Anna's rationalist argument. Translation preserves attribution and does not present them as factual claims.

The speech uses now-outdated racial labels while describing the reach of English publishing. English uses neutral modern wording without modifying the Tamil source.

Claims about technologies, inventors, historical persecution, circulation numbers and maritime history require independent verification before citation.
